    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "It sits at the intersection of state routes 26 and 31, 2.7 miles southeast of Vernon village.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vernon_Center%2C_New_York"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Vernon Center Green Historic District was listed in 1985 as a rare surviving example of an eighteenth-century New England village green.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vernon_Center%2C_New_York"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The district includes a Victorian Gazebo built in 1901, the Green, Parkside United Methodist Church, and the former Presbyterian Church now called This Old Church.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vernon_Center%2C_New_York"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The first outdoor sermon in Vernon Center was delivered in fall 1799 by Rev. Samuel Kirkland, missionary to the Oneidas and founder of Hamilton College.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vernon_Center%2C_New_York"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Vernon Center sits 800 feet above sea level, offering a modest elevation over the surrounding Oneida County landscape.",
